    transferProfile: { audiences: ["existing customers", "product users", "specialist communities"], goals: ["discover customer problems", "improve products", "build a feedback relationship", "prioritize ideas"], constraints: ["moderation", "evaluation capacity", "expectation management", "idea diversity", "privacy"], scale: ["established company", "online community", "small bounded pilot"], geographies: ["global", "online"], businessModels: ["technology", "product business", "customer community"] },
    convention: "Customer suggestions arrive as isolated support requests or research findings, while product decisions remain invisible to the people who proposed them.",
    choice: "Dell created IdeaStorm as a dedicated online route for customers to submit product ideas and changes, with an internal process to review and implement selected suggestions. In a 2010 article, Dell's CMO said the company had implemented about 400 community suggestions.",
    mechanism: "A visible, bounded intake and review path can turn scattered feedback into a searchable decision queue and show customers that some input receives action. The ongoing community also creates selection and workload problems; votes or submissions alone are not demand, revenue, or product value.",
    distinctiveness: "The mechanism connects public idea contribution to an explicit company decision process, rather than treating engagement itself as the outcome. Academic analysis of IdeaStorm also found challenges in sustaining diverse, valuable ideas from repeat contributors.",
    outcome: "Dell's CMO reported about 400 implemented suggestions in 2010. A peer-reviewed 2013 analysis of IdeaStorm found repeat ideators were more likely to produce an idea later implemented, but less likely to repeat that success and more likely to propose ideas similar to those already implemented. These findings do not establish sales or retention impact.",
    evidenceClass: "Company-reported implementation count plus peer-reviewed analysis of community behavior; no causal estimate of business performance.",
    causalLimit: "The implementation count is Dell-reported and does not say how many ideas generated material product or commercial value. The academic study observes selected community data and does not show that IdeaStorm caused sales, loyalty, or product-market success.",
    conditions: ["Customers have problems or ideas the business can meaningfully evaluate", "The business can respond with review criteria and a realistic cadence", "The owner can close the loop on accepted, declined, and deferred ideas", "The intake does not replace support, privacy, safety, or accessibility review"],
    transferRisk: "An unstaffed idea board can create disappointment, moderation costs, and pressure to select popular rather than valuable ideas. Repeat participation can narrow idea diversity; public submissions may expose confidential or sensitive details.",
    smallBusinessAdaptation: "Invite a small, permissioned customer group to submit problems or improvement ideas around one decision. Publish the question, review criteria, decision date, and what the business can realistically change; acknowledge ideas without promising implementation.",
    reversibleTest: "Run one short, private feedback round with an existing customer group. Track distinct problems discovered, ideas reviewed, decisions completed, time spent, and any product change's relevant customer outcome. Compare with the current interview or support method; stop if participation creates more expectation or workload than useful evidence.",
    editorialStatus: "verified_primary_and_secondary", researchConfidence: "medium", accessedAt: "2026-10-25",
    sources: [
      { title: "Keeping Customers Close: Serve your customers through the art of conversation", url: "https://i.dell.com/sites/csdocuments/Corporate_bios_Documents/en/A10-AN1-004%20FinalSayRP.pdf", publisher: "Dell; ANA Magazine", publicationDate: "2010-04", accessedAt: "2026-10-25", sourceRole: "company_primary", claimRole: "action_and_outcome", supports: "Dell's CMO describes IdeaStorm as a channel for customer ideas about products or changes and reports about 400 community suggestions implemented by April 2010; this is company-reported and not a sales-effect estimate." },
      { title: "Crowdsourcing New Product Ideas over Time: An Analysis of the Dell IdeaStorm Community", url: "https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.1120.1599", publisher: "Management Science / INFORMS", publicationDate: "2013-01", accessedAt: "2026-10-25", sourceRole: "independent_secondary", claimRole: "context", supports: "Peer-reviewed study reports that repeat ideators were more likely than one-time ideators to have an idea selected for implementation, but less likely to repeat early success; it highlights diminishing idea diversity and does not establish commercial effects." },
    ],
